module extdeps.tools.pin

import std.types { NonEmptyStr, String, Bool, Int, ContentHash }
import extdeps.external_authority { ExternalAuthority }
import extdeps.uri { Uri, Https }
import extdeps.version { VersionIdentity, VersionConstraint }
import extdeps.tools { CliTool }

data extdeps_external_authority_anchor: ExternalAuthority = ExternalAuthority {
  uri: Uri {
    scheme: Https
    locator: "doc.rust-lang.org/cargo/guide/cargo-toml-vs-cargo-lock.html"
  }
}

data tool_pin_citation_note: String = "Cites Cargo's lockfile discipline — an exact resolved version plus a recorded checksum, with re-resolution an explicit reviewable action rather than a silent float — which is the practice this module models and the precedent tool_pin_selection_note already names. review 44329 correctly found the first version copying extdeps.tools' locator verbatim, forking one source into two authorities; sibling modules each cite their OWN upstream (extdeps.tools.jq to jqlang.org, extdeps.rustup to the rustup book) and this one now does too. The reviewer's suggested remedy — reference the hub's anchor instead of re-spelling it — is NOT available: src/v2/lens/mandatory_tag reads the anchor structurally from the Node tree and an aliased declaration reds corpus_live_clean_tree_wall_holds. Measured, not asserted."

data tool_pin_authority_note: String = "P1 of the hermetic tool-provisioning lane (docs/plans/hermetic-tool-provisioning-design.md). A mechanical tool dependency is a PIN — an exact version identity plus the expected content identity of the binary — never a location and never a range. Both fields are REQUIRED, so a digest-less or range-only pin is unwritable rather than validated (DESIGN section 5 construction, not a post-check). This module deliberately mints NO new digest concept: expected_identity is the same std.types.ContentHash that extdeps.realization.emit_on_demand_host's ResolvedBuildContext.toolchain_identity already carries for the OBSERVED side (#7388, which digests logical name + executable bytes + version-probe output). Pin declares expected, #7388 observes actual, and P2's membership_reconcile value_eq is the comparison between them — one identity concept with two ends, not a second authority. No consumer reads this module yet; P1 is model-before-implement."

data tool_pin_selection_note: String = "The refresh axis, which keeps a pin from being a freeze. Execution ALWAYS resolves an exact identity — there is no moving reference at run time, because resolving 'latest' at execution is nondeterminism at the substrate boundary (v2.std.determinism) and would make two runs of one spec disagree. How the exact identity was CHOSEN is the separable fact: ExactPin is authored directly; TrackedChannel records that the identity was resolved FROM a moving reference (a release channel, 'latest', a semver range) on a stated date, with a declared refresh window. Re-resolution is an explicit action producing a reviewable diff — the Cargo.lock / flake.lock shape. That is the INTENT of this axis; P1 records the policy but cannot yet decide it for either variant — see integrity_versus_currency_note, and the two tracked dissolve-on rows above for the flat-scalar unit and the missing resolution evidence. Nothing here reports a pin current."

data tracked_channel_resolution_date_absent_note: String = "TrackedChannel carries the refresh WINDOW but NOT the resolution DATE, and that is a declared gap rather than a design choice — see pin_currency_gap_note below for why the window is consequently unusable today. An earlier version of this note argued the date was rightly omitted because no fold read it; review 44242 showed that reasoning was backwards. The date is the EVIDENCE, and the correct response to nothing reading it was to make the fold read it, not to drop it. Without the date there is no fact against which currency can be established, which is exactly why every TrackedChannel pin now refuses instead of being decided by a caller-supplied number. refresh_window_days is therefore CARRIED BUT UNREAD today, and retained deliberately rather than dropped: it is the authored refresh POLICY, the half of the decision this PR can legitimately record, and deleting it would discard stated intent and repeat the same mistake in the other direction. Its companion evidence and the arithmetic land together — tracked at tool_pin_tracked_freshness_evidence_dissolve_on, with the flat-scalar unit tracked separately at tool_pin_refresh_window_measure_dissolve_on."

data tool_pin_refresh_window_measure_dissolve_on: String = "feature:tool-pin-refresh-window-measure — DISSOLVE-ON: a calendar-day carrier exists to consume (dag/std/measure.dag stops at Minute today: Nanosecond, Microsecond, Millisecond, Second, Minute — there is no Day, and a calendar day is not an SI scale of seconds, so this cannot be satisfied by picking an existing row); then refresh_window_days becomes that carrier and this row deletes. Consume-never-fork: do NOT mint a Day wrapper here in extdeps — that would be the section 3 fork this lane exists to remove (same discipline as std.measure amortization_months_dissolve_on, whose own note ends 'do not mint a third month wrapper'). Tracked rather than prose so the flat scalar is countable while it stands."

data tool_pin_tracked_freshness_evidence_dissolve_on: String = "feature:tool-pin-tracked-freshness-evidence — DISSOLVE-ON: v2.std.datetime gains day arithmetic (it has no day-difference, epoch-day, or days-between fn today, and zero CalendarDate constructors in use anywhere in the corpus) AND TrackedChannel carries resolved_on: CalendarDate; then pin_currency_gap gains a genuine positive arm derived from stored evidence plus an observed date, refresh_window_days becomes load-bearing, and TrackedChannel becomes eligible for integrity admission. Until both land, pin_currency_gap always names a gap and every TrackedChannel pin refuses admission — see integrity_versus_currency_note."

type PinSelection
  = ExactPin
  | TrackedChannel {
      channel: NonEmptyStr
      refresh_window_days: Int
    }

type ToolPin {
  tool_name: NonEmptyStr
  version: VersionIdentity
  expected_identity: ContentHash
  selection: PinSelection
}

data integrity_versus_currency_note: String = "CORRECTED after review 44274. Two questions were fused under the word 'fresh', and separating them is the whole of this fix. INTEGRITY asks: is the binary in front of us the one this pin declares? That is decidable NOW — compare the observed digest to expected_identity — and it is what admission establishes. CURRENCY asks: is the declared version still the one we should be running? That is NOT decidable here for EITHER variant, and the earlier shape fabricated a positive answer for one of them: ExactPin returned PinFresh unconditionally. Authorship proves reproducibility, not currency — an exact pin authored three years ago is exactly as reproducible and exactly as rotten, and reporting it fresh forever is the 'pinned and never updated' failure this lane's refresh axis exists to prevent (operator's framing when the axis was requested: 'some way to avoid pinning and never updating'). So no arm anywhere returns a positive currency verdict: pin_currency_gap is TOTAL and always names a gap, and the word 'fresh' is gone from the module. A consumer that needs currency reads that gap and refuses; it cannot be handed a green it did not earn."

type CurrencyGap
  = AuthoredPinHasNoReviewDate
  | TrackedPinHasNoResolutionDate { channel: NonEmptyStr }

data pin_currency_gap_note: String = "CORRECTED after review 44242, which found a real fail-open in the first shape and was right. That version stored no resolution evidence on TrackedChannel and took age_days as a CALLER-SUPPLIED parameter, so the freshness verdict was entirely at the caller's discretion: passing 0 forever admitted a permanently-stale pin, and the design note meanwhile claimed the resolution was 'recorded'. That is DESIGN section 5's exact tell — a check satisfiable by editing the caller while the model lies — and deleting the resolution date as an 'unread field' was the wrong correction; the right one is to make the fold READ it. Grounding it properly needs two things this tree does not have: a carried resolution date, and calendar day arithmetic to derive an age from it. v2.std.datetime models CalendarDate but has NO day-difference, epoch-day, or days-between fn, and has ZERO constructors in use anywhere in the corpus, so deriving an age here would mean both becoming that type's first consumer and forking calendar arithmetic into extdeps — a section 3 fork of a std concern. So P1 does NOT offer a verdict it cannot ground: the fabricable parameter is deleted outright and a TrackedChannel pin is UNDERIVABLE, never fresh, so it can never be admitted. ExactPin is unaffected and remains fully usable (P3's Rust toolchain is an exact pin). Dissolve-on: v2.std.datetime gains day arithmetic and TrackedChannel carries resolved_on: CalendarDate, at which point this arm becomes a real fresh/stale decision derived from stored evidence and an observed date."

fn pin_currency_gap(pin: ToolPin) -> CurrencyGap {
  match pin.selection {
    ExactPin => AuthoredPinHasNoReviewDate
    TrackedChannel { channel: c, refresh_window_days: _ } =>
      TrackedPinHasNoResolutionDate { channel: c }
  }
}

type PinRefusalCause
  = ConstraintIsNotIdentity { constraint: VersionConstraint }
  | PinAbsent
  | TrackedResolutionUnrecorded { channel: NonEmptyStr }
  | ObservedIdentityMismatch { expected: ContentHash, observed: ContentHash }

type PinAdmission
  = PinIntegrityAdmitted { pin: ToolPin }
  | PinRefused { tool_name: NonEmptyStr, cause: PinRefusalCause }

data legacy_ingest_none_spelling_blocker_note: String = "DISCOVERED BY EXECUTION while witnessing admit_legacy_cli_tool, and declared rather than dodged (DESIGN section 5: noticing a workaround IS the line-stop signal). The Absent arm below does NOT match a field written with the `none` literal: a `none`-valued optional evaluates to Value::Null and the match reports `non-exhaustive pattern match on: null`, while a field written `Absent` matches fine. This is a live instance of the documented Value::Null open thread — Value::Null is the overloaded None/Absent/miss sentinel — surfaced here by a new consumer. It MATTERS because the corpus overwhelmingly uses the `none` spelling: roughly 568 `: none,` fields against 98 `: Absent,` in dag/ at authoring, and every shipped CliTool row with no version (extdeps.tools.jq, .grep, .sed, .sha256sum, .sleep) is spelled `none`. So this fn is correct on the model and CANNOT yet be run against the real rows: the witness beside it exercises the Absent spelling only, and that limit is stated rather than papered over — a green witness here does not claim corpus coverage. P2 BLOCKER, not P1 debt: ingesting the actual roster needs the Value::Null carrier split (docs/plans/value-null-split.md) or an equivalent grounding of the two spellings onto one representation. Dissolve-on: that split lands, at which point the witness extends to a `none`-spelled row and this note deletes."

data legacy_cli_tool_ingest_note: String = "The discriminating RED for P1, and the migration frontier made countable. EVERY CliTool row in the corpus today refuses conversion to a ToolPin, and that is the correct answer rather than a gap: min_version is extdeps.version.VersionConstraint — a RANGE brand ('>= 7.68') — and a range is not an identity. Two hosts satisfying one range run two different binaries, which is precisely the variance a pin exists to remove, so widening the range into a fabricated identity here would be the absorbing fallback DESIGN section 5 forbids. Absent refuses for the same reason with a distinct cause: no version stated at all is a different deficit from a stated range, and collapsing the two would be a state-space conflation. Each refusal is typed, names its tool, and is countable, so the migration burns down visibly under P4 instead of being silently assumed complete."

fn admit_legacy_cli_tool(tool: CliTool) -> PinAdmission {
  match tool.min_version {
    Present { value: c } =>
      PinRefused {
        tool_name: tool.name,
        cause: ConstraintIsNotIdentity { constraint: c }
      }
    Absent =>
      PinRefused { tool_name: tool.name, cause: PinAbsent }
  }
}

data pin_integrity_admission_note: String = "Establishes INTEGRITY ONLY, and is named so it cannot be misread as currency (review 44274). PinIntegrityAdmitted asserts exactly one thing: the observed binary is the one this pin declares. It asserts NOTHING about whether the declared version is still the right one — that is pin_currency_gap, which is total and always names a gap, so currency can never be silently inherited from an integrity pass. A TrackedChannel pin refuses integrity admission outright (review 44242): its expected_identity was resolved from a moving reference and no record of that resolution is carried, so there is no evidence the stored digest corresponds to any decision anyone made. ExactPin proceeds to the digest comparison, which is the real, decidable check. Both arms refuse rather than widen; neither substitutes a superset, silently re-resolves, nor proceeds on mismatch."

fn admit_pin_integrity(pin: ToolPin, observed_identity: ContentHash) -> PinAdmission {
  match pin.selection {
    TrackedChannel { channel: c, refresh_window_days: _ } =>
      PinRefused {
        tool_name: pin.tool_name,
        cause: TrackedResolutionUnrecorded { channel: c }
      }
    ExactPin =>
      if pin.expected_identity == observed_identity {
        PinIntegrityAdmitted { pin: pin }
      } else {
        PinRefused {
          tool_name: pin.tool_name,
          cause: ObservedIdentityMismatch {
            expected: pin.expected_identity,
            observed: observed_identity
          }
        }
      }
  }
}

data pin_value_eq_note: String = "The bundle member P2 hands to gunbc.membership_reconcile as value_eq. Identity-vs-content split per that spine's authority note: key_of is the TOOL NAME (stable identity), so a version, digest, or selection change is a Modified hunk and re-upserts — an ensure that reinstalls in place — rather than a Removed plus Added pair that would tear the tool down and reinstall it. value_eq is therefore presence-plus-content over every field that changes what gets installed or when it is re-resolved: the pinned version, the expected identity, AND the selection policy. Selection is content, not metadata: flipping ExactPin to TrackedChannel, or widening a refresh window, changes the ensure behaviour with no version or digest movement, so omitting it would emit no hunk for a real drift (review 44329). Selection is compared with the substrate's structural equality on the coproduct rather than a hand-written per-variant predicate — the closed vocabulary already decides it, so a predicate would be a second representation of a decision the substrate makes (review 44341)."

fn pin_value_eq(a: ToolPin, b: ToolPin) -> Bool {
  (a.version == b.version)
    && (a.expected_identity == b.expected_identity)
    && (a.selection == b.selection)
}

fn pin_key_of(pin: ToolPin) -> NonEmptyStr {
  pin.tool_name
}
